-- title:
--   Existence of a full annulus datum at level one
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ with $5 \le q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $q$, a ring homomorphism $red : A \to k$, modular polynomial data `data` for $q$ satisfying the Kronecker congruence `hKr`, and integrality of the two Hecke degeneracy embeddings at level $1$ and prime $q$; let $P$ be a place specialisation for these data, $W$ a finite set of places of the geometric modular function field `modularFunctionFieldC k 1` whose members are exactly the supersingular places `ssPlaces q 1 k`, and $R$ a prolongation tuple for $P$ satisfying `IsModel`, `RegularityLaw W`, `NodeValueLaw W`, `OrderLawFixed` and `ValueIntegralityLaw` at each $w \in W$. Then there is an annulus datum `dat : R.AnnulusDatumQ W` — that is, number fields $K_w \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, node coordinates $(x_w, y_w)$ in `R.nodeIntegersOver (K w) w` for $w \in W$, widths $e_w \in \mathbb{N}$, a depth function $\delta$ on the places of `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * q)` with values in $\mathbb{Q}$, a cusp place, elements $\pi_w, \pi'_w$ of the function field and units $\bar u_0(w), \lambda_w, \mu_w$ of $k$ — with all of the following. First, $1 \le e_w$ and $e_w =$ `jWidth` of the value of `jGeomGen k 1` at $w$ (namely $3$, $2$ or $1$ according as that value is $0$, $1728$ or neither), for $w \in W$. Second, for $w \in W$ and every place $V$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * q)` with `P.reduceFst V = w` which is neither `P.IsStrictFst` nor `P.IsStrictSnd`, one has $0 < \delta(V) < e_w$ and the cleared relation $\mathrm{val}_A(V(y_w))^{\,\mathrm{den}\,\delta(V)} = \mathrm{val}_A(q)^{\,\mathrm{num}\,\delta(V)}$ in the value group of $A$. Third, $\delta$ is invariant under the action, through `arithmeticGalois`, of the inertia subgroup `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`. Fourth, the cusp lies outside $W$, is fixed by the arithmetic Frobenius `arithFrobC q k 1`, and for $w \in W$ the divisors of $\pi_w$ and $\pi'_w$ are $[w] - [\mathrm{cusp}]$ and $[\varphi w] - [\mathrm{cusp}]$ respectively, where $\varphi =$ `arithFrobC q k 1`, stated as equality of coefficients with orders at every place. Fifth, every element of $K_w$ is fixed by each element of the inertia subgroup, for $w \in W$, and $K_w$ is finite over $\mathbb{Q}$ for every place $w$. Finally, there are elements $\varpi_w, \varepsilon_w$ of the coefficient ring $A \cap K_w$, natural numbers $e_{K_w}$, and elements $u_w$ of `R.nodeIntegersOver (K w) w` such that, for $w \in W$: the kernel of the reduction `NodeLocalized.redRestrict red (K w)` consists exactly of the multiples of $\varpi_w$; $1 \le e_{K_w}$; $\varepsilon_w$ is a unit with $q = \varpi_w^{e_{K_w}} \varepsilon_w$ and reduction $1$; $u_w$ is a unit and $x_w y_w = \varpi_w^{\,e_w e_{K_w}} u_w$ with $\varpi_w$ entering through `R.nodeConst`; the ideal generated by $\varpi_w, x_w, y_w$ is maximal and is the only maximal ideal of the node ring; the ideals generated by $\varpi_w, x_w$ and by $\varpi_w, y_w$ are prime, with $y_w$ outside the first and $x_w$ outside the second; the node ring is Noetherian; every element $g$ of it admits $o \in A \cap K_w$ with $g - \mathrm{nodeConst}(o)$ a non-unit; and the residues are read off as values, $\bar u_0(w)$ for $u_w$ at $w$ via `R.nodeResidue₁`, $\lambda_w$ for $y_w/\pi_w$ at $w$, and $\mu_w$ for $x_w/\pi'_w$ at $\varphi w$ via `R.nodeResidue₂`.
--
--   This packages, at level one and for $q \ge 5$, the local structure of the reduction of $X_0(q)$ at a supersingular point: the two branches cross in an ordinary double point, the node ring has the crossing presentation $xy = \varpi^{e e_K} u$ over a number field fixed by inertia, and the places above the node are measured by a rational depth lying strictly inside the annulus of width given by the $j$-width of the centre. It is the single datum-producing statement feeding [`ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.exists_fixedStrict_kernelGood_principal`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.exists_fixedStrict_kernelGood_principal), each conjunct matching a hypothesis of that consumer.
